<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#jersey-royal">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jersey Royal</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1880</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Jersey Royal potato</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Jersey Royal is a variety of early new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potato">potato</a> grown in Jersey, developed around 1880 by the farmer Hugh de la Haye from an unusually large potato with many eyes. Grown on the island's sloping south-facing côtils, it is exported to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> each spring and became the chief cash crop of Jersey farming. It has protected designation of origin status in the United Kingdom.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jersey-royal.jpg" alt="Jersey Royal" width="320" height="272" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Man vyi · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jersey_Royal_potatoes.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
